BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 50 760 Td (What dynamic programming is) Tj T* () Tj T* (Dynamic programming is a technique for solving a problem by combining the solutions to) Tj T* (smaller subproblems, where the same subproblems come up again and again. Instead of) Tj T* (recomputing a subproblem every time it is needed, we solve it once and store the result in) Tj T* (a table.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A problem is a good candidate for dynamic programming when it has two properties.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Overlapping subproblems: a naive recursive solution solves the same subproblem many times.) Tj T* (This is what makes storing results pay off. Merge sort, by contrast, has subproblems that) Tj T* (never repeat, so it gains nothing from a table.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Optimal substructure: an optimal solution to the whole problem contains optimal solutions) Tj T* (to its subproblems. This is what makes the recurrence correct.) Tj T* () Tj T* (There are two ways to implement a dynamic program. Memoization is the top-down approach:) Tj T* (write the natural recursion, and before computing a subproblem check whether its answer is) Tj T* (already in the table. Tabulation is the bottom-up approach: fill the table iteratively,) Tj T* (starting from the base cases, in an order that guarantees every entry's dependencies are) Tj T* (filled before the entry itself.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Memoization only solves the subproblems that are actually reached, and it keeps the shape) Tj T* (of the recursion. Tabulation has no recursion overhead, cannot overflow the call stack,) Tj T* (and makes it easier to reduce memory by keeping only the rows that are still needed.) Tj T* ET
endstream
endobj
