BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 50 760 Td (Longest common subsequence) Tj T* () Tj T* (A subsequence of a string is obtained by deleting zero or more characters without changing) Tj T* (the order of the rest. The longest common subsequence problem, or LCS, asks for the) Tj T* (longest sequence that is a subsequence of both of two given strings X of length m and Y of) Tj T* (length n. For example, the LCS of ABCBDAB and BDCABA has length 4; one such subsequence is) Tj T* (BCBA.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Subproblem: let L[i][j] be the length of the LCS of the first i characters of X and the) Tj T* (first j characters of Y.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Base cases: L[i][0] = 0 for all i, and L[0][j] = 0 for all j, because an empty string has) Tj T* (no common subsequence with anything.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Recurrence: if X[i] equals Y[j], then L[i][j] = L[i-1][j-1] + 1. Otherwise, L[i][j] is the) Tj T* (larger of L[i-1][j] and L[i][j-1].) Tj T* () Tj T* (Fill order: row by row, left to right, so that the three entries each cell depends on are) Tj T* (already known.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The table has \(m+1\) times \(n+1\) entries and each takes constant time, so the running time) Tj T* (is O\(mn\) and the space is O\(mn\). If only the length is needed, two rows are enough, which) Tj T* (reduces the space to O\(min\(m, n\)\).) Tj T* () Tj T* (To recover the subsequence itself, trace back from L[m][n]: when the characters match,) Tj T* (take that character and move diagonally; otherwise move toward the larger neighbor.) Tj T* ET
endstream
