BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 50 760 Td (The 0/1 knapsack problem) Tj T* () Tj T* (We are given n items. Item i has a weight w_i and a value v_i. The knapsack can carry a) Tj T* (total weight of at most W. Each item is either taken whole or left behind, which is why) Tj T* (the problem is called 0/1. The goal is to choose items with the largest total value whose) Tj T* (total weight does not exceed W.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A greedy strategy that takes items in order of value per unit weight does not always work) Tj T* (for 0/1 knapsack. It is only guaranteed to be optimal for the fractional knapsack problem,) Tj T* (where items can be split.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Subproblem: let K[i][w] be the largest value achievable using only the first i items with) Tj T* (a knapsack of capacity w.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Base cases: K[0][w] = 0 for every w, because with no items the value is zero.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Recurrence: if w_i is greater than w, item i cannot fit, so K[i][w] = K[i-1][w].) Tj T* (Otherwise, K[i][w] is the larger of K[i-1][w], which skips item i, and K[i-1][w - w_i] +) Tj T* (v_i, which takes it.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The table has \(n+1\) times \(W+1\) entries, so the running time is O\(nW\). This is called) Tj T* (pseudo-polynomial time: it is polynomial in the numeric value of W, but W is written with) Tj T* (only about log W bits, so the running time is exponential in the size of the input. The) Tj T* (0/1 knapsack problem is NP-hard, and this algorithm does not contradict that.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The space can be reduced from O\(nW\) to O\(W\) by keeping a single row and filling it from) Tj T* (right to left, so that each item is counted at most once.) Tj T* ET
endstream
